<section class="chapter" aria-label="What decision logic is">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">What decision logic is</h2>

    <p class="muted">
      Decision logic is the layer that translates <strong>shared signals</strong> into
      <strong>recommended actions, routing, monitoring, or escalation</strong>.
      It sits between measurement and execution.
    </p>

    <p>
      Without decision logic, teams can see the same pattern and still act inconsistently.
      With explicit logic, the system can interpret comparable inputs in comparable ways.
    </p>

    <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4" aria-label="Decision logic principles">
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Explicit</b>
        <span>Rules are written down instead of living in tribal knowledge.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Reviewable</b>
        <span>People can inspect why an action or recommendation occurred.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Bounded</b>
        <span>Logic determines where AI can monitor, recommend, or act.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Consistent</b>
        <span>The same signal pattern should not create different outcomes by person or mood.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      Signals make patterns visible. Decision logic determines what those patterns mean operationally.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" aria-label="How decision logic works">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">How decision logic works</h2>

    <p class="muted">
      The logic layer usually follows a simple sequence: detect, interpret, route, and log.
    </p>

    <div class="grid3" aria-label="Logic flow">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Detect</h3>
        <p class="muted">The system identifies movement in a signal or threshold condition.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Metric crosses expected range</li>
          <li>Behavior pattern repeats</li>
          <li>Variance or drift becomes visible</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Interpret</h3>
        <p class="muted">The system applies shared definitions and logic to determine what the pattern likely means.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Capacity bottleneck</li>
          <li>Decision inconsistency</li>
          <li>Execution hygiene problem</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Route</h3>
        <p class="muted">The system decides whether to monitor, recommend, auto-execute, or escalate.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Low-risk work moves automatically</li>
          <li>Higher-risk actions require review</li>
          <li>High-stakes decisions always route to humans</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" aria-label="Core components of decision logic">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Core components of decision logic</h2>

    <div class="grid2" aria-label="Decision logic components">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Thresholds</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          Thresholds define when a signal should trigger attention, intervention, or escalation.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Expected ranges</li>
          <li>Alert triggers</li>
          <li>Escalation boundaries</li>
          <li>Minimum acceptable conditions</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Routing Rules</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          Routing rules define where work goes once a condition is met.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Who owns the next step</li>
          <li>Whether action is automated or approved</li>
          <li>What context accompanies the handoff</li>
          <li>What gets logged for auditability</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid2" aria-label="Decision logic components continued">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Decision Rights</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          Not every action belongs to the same actor. Decision rights make authority explicit.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>AI may observe, recommend, or act</li>
          <li>Humans retain authority where stakes are material</li>
          <li>Approvals are structured rather than improvised</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Receipts</h3>
        <p class="muted">
          Every recommendation or action should be traceable to the signal and logic that produced it.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Why this surfaced</li>
          <li>What threshold was crossed</li>
          <li>What action was taken</li>
          <li>What happened next</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" aria-label="Types of decision logic">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Types of decision logic</h2>

    <p class="muted">
      Different signal types require different logic. Operational issues, behavioral patterns,
      and decision-quality drift do not route the same way.
    </p>

    <div class="grid3" aria-label="Logic types">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Operational Logic</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Detects bottlenecks, delays, capacity strain, and missed SLAs</li>
          <li>Routes to recruiter, coordinator, or hiring manager workflow owners</li>
          <li>Often suitable for low-risk automation</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Behavioral Logic</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Interprets communication quality, responsiveness, and execution hygiene</li>
          <li>Supports review, coaching, or structured follow-up</li>
          <li>Often needs human judgement before action</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <h3>Decision Integrity Logic</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Detects calibration drift, rubric override, or inconsistency across reviewers</li>
          <li>Routes to review, calibration, or threshold adjustment</li>
          <li>High-stakes use cases require explicit governance</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" aria-label="Autonomy and escalation">
  <div class="chapter__inner">
    <h2 class="section-title">Autonomy and escalation</h2>

    <p class="muted">
      Decision logic should make clear where automation is safe and where escalation is required.
    </p>

    <div class="miniGrid miniGrid--4" aria-label="Autonomy tiers">
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 0 — Monitor</b>
        <span>Surface the signal. No action is taken.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 1 — Auto-execute</b>
        <span>Low-risk reminders, nudges, routing, and hygiene actions.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 2 — Recommend</b>
        <span>AI proposes an action, but a human approves it.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="mini">
        <b>Tier 3 — Escalate</b>
        <span>High-stakes outcomes always route to humans.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      Good decision logic does not maximize automation. It maximizes safe, explainable, and accountable action.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

